# Concurrent C++ Code Review Checklist

## 1. Data Races and Thread Safety

### 1.1 Unsynchronized Shared Mutable State
**Check**: Look for shared variables accessed by multiple threads without proper synchronization.

**Example Failure**:
```cpp
// BAD: Shared counter without synchronization
int global_counter = 0;

void increment() {
    global_counter++; // Data race!
}

// Multiple threads calling increment() simultaneously
```

**Fix**:
```cpp
std::atomic<int> global_counter{0};
// or use mutex
std::mutex mtx;
void increment() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    global_counter++;
}
```

### 1.2 Incorrect Mutex Usage
**Check**: Verify mutexes are properly locked/unlocked and scoped correctly.

**Example Failure**:
```cpp
std::mutex mtx;
int shared_data = 0;

void bad_function() {
    mtx.lock();
    if (shared_data > 0) {
        return; // Forgot to unlock! Deadlock potential
    }
    shared_data++;
    mtx.unlock(); // Only reached if condition is false
}
```

**Fix**:
```cpp
void good_function() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    if (shared_data > 0) {
        return; // Lock automatically released
    }
    shared_data++;
} // Lock automatically released
```

## 2. Deadlock Prevention

### 2.1 Circular Lock Ordering
**Check**: Ensure consistent lock ordering across all code paths.

**Example Failure**:
```cpp
std::mutex mtx1, mtx2;

// Thread 1
void func1() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock1(mtx1);
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock2(mtx2); // May deadlock
}

// Thread 2
void func2() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock2(mtx2);
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock1(mtx1); // Opposite order!
}
```

**Fix**:
```cpp
void func1() {
    std::lock(mtx1, mtx2); // Locks in consistent order
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock1(mtx1, std::adopt_lock);
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock2(mtx2, std::adopt_lock);
}
```

### 2.2 Self-Deadlock
**Check**: Look for recursive locking or re-entrant calls.

**Example Failure**:
```cpp
std::mutex mtx;

void problematic_function() {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    if (some_condition()) {
        problematic_function(); // Self-deadlock!
    }
}
```

